e 3" formation, meaning that if White plays at either end of this line (specifically at F9 or J5), they will create an "Open 4." An Open 4 is a sequence of four stones with both ends unobstructed, guaranteeing a win on the following turn because the opponent can only block one side. Black has no immediate winning sequences (such as a Series of Fours) to counter-attack faster than White\'s threat. Therefore, Black is forced to block this diagonal immediately.\n\nReason: Black must play at either F9 or J5 to prevent White from creating an Open 4. Comparing the two options, **J5** is the superior move. J5 is located in a tactical "hot zone" adjacent to other White stones (I6 and J6). By placing a stone at J5, Black not only blocks the lethal diagonal threat but also disrupts White\'s local structure, preventing them from forming strong connections or shapes (like a cluster around J5/J6/I6) in subsequent turns. Blocking at F9 would leave the vital J5 point available for White to occupy later, which would grant them a strong positional advantage. Thus, J5 maximizes defensive value and positional disruption.\n\nAnswer: {\n  "col": "J",\n  "row": 5\n}'}
