ture.\nThe reason is that there is an Open 3 at G8,G9,G10 that needs to be blocked to prevent an immediate loss.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Based on the analysis of the board:\n\n1.  **Threat Assessment:**\n    *   **White (O)** has a vertical line of three stones at **G8, G9, G10**.\n    *   The spots **G7** and **G11** are currently empty.\n    *   The spot **G6** is empty.\n    *   This formation is a vertical **Open 3** (or Jump 3 depending on perspective, but effectively creates an Open 4).\n    *   If White plays at **G7**, they will have 4 continuous stones (G7-G10) with open ends at G6 and G11. This is an **Open 4**, which is an unstoppable winning threat (White will play the open end on the next turn to make 5).\n    *   Similarly, if White plays at **G11**, they will have G8-G11, which is also an Open 4 (ends at G7 and G12).\n\n2.  **Black\'s Status:**\n    *   Black (X) has several clusters (J7-J10, H6-K9 diagonal), but they are all "dead" (blocked at one or both ends) or do not form an immediate winning threat (4-in-a-row or Open 3) that is faster than White\'s threat.\n    *   For example, Black\'s diagonal H6-I7-J8-K9 is blocked by G5 and L10.\n    *   Black\'s vertical J7-J10 is blocked by J6 and J11.\n    *   Playing K7 creates an Open 3 (K7-K9) and a blocked 3 (I7-K7), but this is not a forcing win sequence (VCF) that outpaces White\'s immediate win.\n\n3.  **Strategy:**\n    *   Black **must** block White\'s threat in Column G immediately.\n    *   The blocking options are **G7** or **G11**.\n    *   **Comparing G7 vs G11:**\n        *   Playing **G7** blocks the line and prevents White from placing a stone there. Crucially, a White stone at G7 would connect with H7(O), creating a stronger local shape. By playing G7, Black occupies this spot, creating potential diagonal connections with H6 and H8.\n        *   Playing **G11** also blocks the line, but allows White to play G7 as a forcing move (creating a blocked 4), which would require Black to answer at G6. This leaves White with a stone at G7 connected to H7.\n    *   Therefore, **G7** is the structurally superior defensive move.\n\nEvaluation: **Forced defense**. Prevents immediate defeat by blocking White\'s Open 3 which would become an unstoppable Open 4.\n\nReason: White has a lethal vertical Open 3 at G8-G10. If White plays G7 or G11, they form an Open 4 and win. Black must block this line. G7 is preferred over G11 because it breaks White\'s potential connection with H7 and keeps Black\'s stones more connected to H6.\n\nAnswer:\n```json\n{"col": "G", "row": 7}\n```'}
